# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_TASK_CREATE_EXCEPTION_CHANNEL */
int64_t task_create_exception_channel(uint32_t task, uint64_t options,
                                      uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_EXCEPTION_GET_THREAD */
int64_t exception_get_thread(uint32_t exception, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_EXCEPTION_GET_PROCESS */
int64_t exception_get_process(uint32_t exception, uint32_t *out);
```

# DESCRIPTION

When a user thread faults, or a job's policy raises an exception, the kernel offers it to exception channels in order: the thread's, its process's, then each job up the tree.
Each offer is a message on the channel: a `croi_exception_info_t` (process koid, thread koid, type) and one handle to an exception object.
The faulting thread waits, in a wait that a kill ends, until the last handle to the exception object is closed.
It then does what the object's state says:

| State | Value | The thread |
| `CROI_EXCEPTION_STATE_TRY_NEXT` | 0 | is offered to the next channel (the state at first) |
| `CROI_EXCEPTION_STATE_HANDLED` | 1 | resumes, with any registers the handler wrote with thread_write_state(2) |
| `CROI_EXCEPTION_STATE_THREAD_EXIT` | 2 | exits |

With no channel left the process is killed, with return code `CROI_TASK_RETCODE_EXCEPTION_KILL`.
A channel whose user end is closed is skipped.

The exception types are `CROI_EXCP_GENERAL`, `CROI_EXCP_FATAL_PAGE_FAULT`, `CROI_EXCP_UNDEFINED_INSTRUCTION`, `CROI_EXCP_SW_BREAKPOINT`, `CROI_EXCP_HW_BREAKPOINT`, `CROI_EXCP_UNALIGNED_ACCESS` and `CROI_EXCP_POLICY_ERROR`.
A debugger channel also gets `CROI_EXCP_THREAD_STARTING`, `CROI_EXCP_THREAD_EXITING` (no registers, and the reply is ignored) and, on a job, `CROI_EXCP_PROCESS_STARTING`.
While a thread is in an exception, object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_THREAD_EXCEPTION_REPORT` gives its `croi_exception_report_t`, and thread_read_state(2) its registers.

The state is read and set as the property `CROI_PROP_EXCEPTION_STATE` (a `uint32_t`) of the exception object with object_get_property(2) and object_set_property(2).
`CROI_PROP_EXCEPTION_STRATEGY` (`CROI_EXCEPTION_STRATEGY_FIRST_CHANCE` 0 or `_SECOND_CHANCE` 1) says whether a debugger sees the exception before the other handlers or after them; it can be set only on an exception that went to a debugger channel (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` otherwise).
The handle to the exception has TRANSFER, DUPLICATE, WAIT, INSPECT, GET_PROPERTY and SET_PROPERTY.

## task_create_exception_channel

`task_create_exception_channel` makes a channel for the exceptions of <task>, a thread, a process or a job, and writes the user end to <out>; the task keeps the other.
The handle <task> needs INSPECT, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and MANAGE_THREAD, and for a process or job also ENUMERATE.
The channel's handle has TRANSFER, WAIT and READ.
<options> is 0 or `CROI_EXCEPTION_CHANNEL_DEBUGGER`, which makes a debugger channel: it also gets a process's start and each thread's start and exit.
A thread has no debugger channel (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
A task has one channel of each kind while the user end is open (`CROI_ERR_ALREADY_BOUND`); a job may have up to `CROI_EXCEPTION_CHANNEL_JOB_DEBUGGER_MAX_COUNT` (32) debugger channels.
The job's policy for new channels applies.

## exception_get_thread, exception_get_process

These write to <out> a handle to the thread that took the exception, or its process, with the full rights of a thread or process.
The handle <exception> must be an exception object; it needs no right.

